  "Question": "The set of \\(x\\)-values satisfying the equation \\[ x^{\\log_{10}x}=\\frac{x^3}{100} \\] consists of:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\dfrac1{10},\\) only",
    "B": "\\(10,\\) only",
    "C": "\\(100,\\) only",
    "D": "\\(10\\) or \\(100,\\) only",
    "E": "more than two real numbers"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "The logarithm requires \\(x>0\\). On this domain both sides are positive, so taking base-ten logarithms is valid and reversible. This turns the variable exponent into a product:\n\\[\n(\\log_{10}x)^2=3\\log_{10}x-2.\n\\]\nLet \\(t=\\log_{10}x\\). Then\n\\[\nt^2-3t+2=(t-1)(t-2)=0,\n\\]\nso \\(t=1\\) or \\(t=2\\). Since \\(x=10^t\\), the only candidates are \\(x=10\\) and \\(x=100\\).\n\nSubstitution into the original equation gives \\(10=10\\) and \\(10000=10000\\), respectively. Thus the solution set is \\(\\{10,100\\}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{D}}\\).",
